    "content": "FORMAT B-17  -  APPLICATION FOR FURTHER INVESTIGATION /\nRE-INVESTIGATION\nPre-Trial / Complaint & FIR\nIN THE COURT OF THE .............................., (DISTRICT ..............................)\nCRIMINAL CASE NO. .......................... OF 20.........\nIN THE MATTER OF:\n...................................................................., S/o / D/o / W/o .............................................., R/o\n.............................................. ........... APPLICANT / COMPLAINANT / VICTIM\n1. THE STATE .............................................. ........... RESPONDENT NO. 1\n2. .............................................. ........... RESPONDENT NO. 2 / ACCUSED\nPOLICE STATION .............................................\nFIR NO. .......................... OF 20.........\nAPPLICATION FOR FURTHER INVESTIGATION UNDER SECTION 193(8) READ WITH\nSECTION 175 AND SECTION 528 OF THE BHARATIYA NAGARIK SURAKSHA SANHITA,\nMOST RESPECTFULLY SHOWETH:\n1. That the applicant is the complainant/victim in the above-noted case, and is interested in the fair and effective\ninvestigation of the offences punishable under Sections .............................. of the Bharatiya Nyaya\nSanhita, 2023, registered vide FIR No. .......................... dated .......................... at Police Station\n..............................\n2. That the investigation in the above-noted case has been completed and a Final Report under Section 193(3),\nBNSS, 2023 has been filed before this Hon'ble Court on .......................... / the investigation is still in\nprogress, and the applicant has come to learn of the following facts which were not investigated:\n(i) ..............................;\n(ii) ..............................;\n(iii) ..............................\n3. That the following material evidence, which is crucial for a just decision of the case, has been left out of the\ninvestigation and is still available for collection:\n(a) .............................. (e.g., the call detail records of the accused for the period .............................. to\n.........................., which will establish the conspiracy and the contact between the accused persons);\n(b) .............................. (the CCTV footage/CCTV camera at the place of occurrence, which was not seized\nwithin the prescribed time, although the applicant specifically pointed out the location of the camera);\n(c) .............................. (the bank account statements and the transaction records, which were not collected);\n(d) .............................. (the statements of the material witnesses, namely .............................., who were present\nat the spot and were not examined);\n(e) the forensic examination of .............................. (the seized article/electronic device/document), which was\nnot sent to the Forensic Science Laboratory.\n4. That the failure to collect the said evidence has prejudiced the applicant and has the effect of defeating the\ncourse of justice, and unless further investigation is directed, the applicant shall be deprived of a fair\ninvestigation and a fair trial, which is a facet of the right to life and personal liberty under Article 21 of the\nConstitution of India.\n\n5. That Section 193(8), BNSS, 2023 provides that nothing in the said section shall be deemed to preclude\nfurther investigation in respect of an offence after a report under Section 193(3) has been forwarded to the\nMagistrate, and where, upon such investigation, the police obtains further evidence, oral or documentary,\nit shall forward a further report or reports to the Magistrate, and such further report shall be treated as a\nreport under Section 193(3), against which the Magistrate may take cognizance.\n6. That Section 175(3), BNSS, 2023 empowers the Magistrate, upon an application of the complainant, to order\ninvestigation, and the said power is not exhausted by one round of investigation.\n7. That this Hon'ble Court also has the power under Section 528, BNSS, 2023 (inherent powers) and under\nSection 348, BNSS, 2023 to summon material witnesses and to make such orders as may be necessary to\nsecure the ends of justice and to prevent the abuse of the process of the Court; the prayer of the applicant is\nsquarely within the scope of those powers.\n8. That the present application is bona fide and the applicant has approached this Hon'ble Court at the earliest\nafter coming to know of the lacunae in the investigation. No prejudice will be caused to the accused by the\ngrant of the relief prayed for, as the accused will have full opportunity to explain the additional material at\nthe trial, and the ends of justice require that the truth be brought out.\n9. That the applicant undertakes to produce before the investigating officer all documents and details within\nhis/her knowledge, and to render all assistance in the further investigation.\nPRAYER:\nIt is, therefore, most respectfully prayed that this Hon'ble Court may kindly be pleased to:\n(i) direct further investigation in the above-noted case, in terms of Section 193(8) read with Section 175,\nBNSS, 2023, and direct the investigating agency to collect, in particular, the material enumerated in\nparagraph 3 above;\n(ii) in the alternative, direct a re-investigation into the matter by an independent agency/officer of the rank of\n.............................. in view of the conduct of the previous investigating officer;\n(iii) direct the investigating officer to file a further report/further Final Report before this Hon'ble Court in a\ntime-bound manner, and to keep this Hon'ble Court informed of the progress of the investigation; and\n(iv) pass such other and further order(s) as this Hon'ble Court may deem fit and proper.\nAPPLICANT / COMPLAINANT\nTHROUGH\nADVOCATE\nPlace: ..............................\nDate: ..............................\nNOTES, PRACTICE POINTS AND DOCUMENTS TO BE ANNEXED\n[Note: (i) The power to direct further investigation is distinct from the power to direct re-investigation. A re-investigation is\nordered only in exceptional cases where the investigation is found to be tainted, biased or fundamentally defective, and the\nrelief should ordinarily be sought before the High Court under Section 528, BNSS, 2023 or Article 226 of the Constitution. (ii)\nFurther investigation after the filing of the Final Report is permissible on the basis of new material, and the Magistrate may\ndiffer from the conclusions of the investigating officer. (iii) Under Section 193(9), BNSS, 2023 (as inserted), further\ninvestigation may be conducted with the prior permission of the Court in respect of offences punishable with imprisonment of\nthree years or more, and with the prior permission of the officer not below the rank of Inspector where the offence is punishable\nwith imprisonment of three years or more but less than seven years. The exact requirement should be verified against the text of\nthe Sanhita as applicable in the State concerned. (iv) The application must set out the specific lacunae; a vague prayer for\nre-investigation is generally not entertained.]\nCR.P.C. EQUIVALENT\nOld Section 173(8), Cr.P.C., 1973 -> Section 193(8), BNSS, 2023. See also Section 175(3), BNSS, 2023 (old Section 156(3))\nand Section 528, BNSS, 2023 (old Section 482).\n\nPRECEDENTS REFERRED TO\n• Vinay Tyagi v. Irshad Ali, (2013) 5 SCC 762 - the power to order further investigation is not subject to any restriction;\nhowever, re-investigation is ordered only in exceptional cases.\n• Ramachandran v. R. Udhayakumar, (2008) 13 SCC 277 - further investigation under Section 173(8) can be ordered even after\nthe filing of the charge-sheet and during the trial.\n• Hemant Dhasmana v. CBI, (2001) 7 SCC 536 - the Magistrate may direct further investigation and the police may submit a\nfurther report.\n• Sakiri Vasu v. State of U.P., (2008) 2 SCC 409 - the police must undertake a fair and thorough investigation; the Court may\nmonitor the investigation.\n• Amish Devgan v. Union of India, (2021) 1 SCC 1 - the Court's power to transfer investigation to an independent agency in\nexceptional cases."
