    "content": "FORMAT E-51  -  APPLICATION FOR DISCHARGE OF PROCLAMATION /\nRECALL OF NON-BAILABLE WARRANT / SETTING ASIDE EX-PARTE ORDER\nTrial Stage\nIN THE COURT OF .............................., (DISTRICT ..............................)\nCRIMINAL CASE NO. .......................... OF 20.........\nIN THE MATTER OF:\n...................................................................., S/o .............................., R/o .............................................. ...........\nAPPLICANT / ACCUSED\nTHE STATE .............................................. ........... RESPONDENT\nFIR NO. .......................... OF 20........., POLICE STATION ..............................\nAPPLICATION FOR RECALL OF THE NON-BAILABLE WARRANT AND FOR SETTING\nASIDE THE PROCLAMATION ORDER UNDER SECTIONS 84, 86 AND 89 OF THE\nBHARATIYA NAGARIK SURAKSHA SANHITA, 2023\nMOST RESPECTFULLY SHOWETH:\n1. That the applicant/accused is facing trial in the above-noted case for the offences punishable under Sections\n.............................. of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023.\n2. That this Hon'ble Court was pleased to issue a non-bailable warrant of arrest against the applicant on\n.........................., and thereafter to issue a proclamation under Section 84, BNSS, 2023 on ..........................,\nand an order of attachment of the property of the applicant under Section 85, BNSS, 2023 on\n.........................., on the ground that the applicant had failed to appear before this Hon'ble Court on the\ndates fixed, namely ..............................\n3. That the applicant was not served with the summons/warrant in the manner prescribed by law, and the\napplicant had no knowledge of the pendency of the proceedings against him/her, in that: (i) the\nsummons/warrant was not served upon the applicant personally; (ii) the report of the process server, dated\n.........................., shows that the summons was affixed on the door of the applicant's residence, although\nthe applicant was not residing at the said address at the relevant time, being at ..............................; (iii) the\napplicant has never evaded the process of law, and the absence of the applicant was neither wilful nor\ndeliberate.\n4. That the applicant has now come to know of the proceedings, and has voluntarily appeared/surrendered\nbefore this Hon'ble Court on .......................... and has moved the present application at the earliest\nopportunity thereafter.\n5. That the applicant's non-appearance was occasioned by the following reasons: (i) .............................. (e.g.,\nthe applicant was out of station on account of employment/business at .............................., and the\nsummons was not brought to the applicant's notice; (ii) the applicant was suffering from a serious illness,\nas is evident from the medical certificate annexed hereto; (iii) the applicant had shifted to a new address,\nnamely .............................., and the change of address was not known to the process-serving agency,\nthough the applicant had, in fact, .............................. (if applicable, mention the intimation of the change of\naddress)).\n6. That the applicant undertakes to appear before this Hon'ble Court on every date of hearing henceforth, and to\nabide by all such conditions as this Hon'ble Court may impose. The applicant also undertakes to furnish\nsuch bond, with or without sureties, as this Hon'ble Court may direct, in terms of Section 92, BNSS, 2023,\nand to remain present on each date of hearing without fail.\n7. That Section 86, BNSS, 2023 provides for the identification and attachment of the property of a proclaimed\nperson, and Section 88, BNSS, 2023 provides for the release, sale and restoration of the attached property;\n\nSection 89, BNSS, 2023 provides for an appeal from an order rejecting an application for the restoration of\nthe attached property. The applicant further submits that the order of attachment of the applicant's\nproperty, namely .............................., deserves to be set aside, as the applicant has appeared before this\nHon'ble Court, and there is no longer any justification for the continuance of the attachment.\n8. That no prejudice whatsoever shall be caused to the prosecution by the recall of the warrant and by the setting\naside of the proclamation/attachment, whereas the applicant would suffer grave and irreparable injury,\nincluding the loss of liberty and of the applicant's property, if the reliefs prayed for are not granted.\nPRAYER:\nIt is, therefore, most respectfully prayed that this Hon'ble Court may kindly be pleased to:\n(i) recall the non-bailable warrant of arrest issued against the applicant on .........................., and the order of\nproclamation issued under Section 84, BNSS, 2023 on .........................., and the order of attachment of\nthe applicant's property issued on ..........................;\n(ii) set aside the order of attachment and direct the release and restoration of the attached property, namely\n.............................., to the applicant, in terms of Section 88, BNSS, 2023;\n(iii) accept the bond with or without sureties to be furnished by the applicant, and permit the applicant to\nappear and to defend the case henceforth, on such terms and conditions as this Hon'ble Court may deem\nfit and proper; and\n(iv) pass such other and further order(s) as this Hon'ble Court may deem fit and proper.\nAPPLICANT / ACCUSED\nTHROUGH\nADVOCATE\nPlace: ..............................\nDate: ..............................\nNOTES, PRACTICE POINTS AND DOCUMENTS TO BE ANNEXED\n[Note: (i) Section 84, BNSS, 2023 (old Section 82, Cr.P.C.) governs the publication of a proclamation against an absconding\nperson; the proclamation must be published as prescribed, and the period must not be less than thirty days. (ii) Section 85,\nBNSS, 2023 (old Section 83) provides for the attachment of the property of a proclaimed person; the Court may attach the\nproperty by seizure or by appointment of a receiver. (iii) Section 86, BNSS, 2023 (old Section 83A) requires an identification\nand attachment of the property of a proclaimed person. (iv) Section 87, BNSS, 2023 (old Section 84) provides for claims and\nobjections to the attachment. (v) Section 88, BNSS, 2023 (old Section 85) provides for the release, sale and restoration of the\nattached property, and requires the Court to release the property in whole or in part if the proclaimed person appears within\ntwo years, and to sell the property if the person does not appear. (vi) The crucial requirement for a valid proclamation is that\nthe person must be shown to be 'absconding' - i.e., he must have absconded to evade the process of law; the mere\nnon-appearance of the accused without the requisite mens rea is not sufficient to make him a proclaimed offender. (vii) The\napplicant should also seek the recall of the order declaring the applicant a 'proclaimed offender', if such an order has been\npassed.]\nCR.P.C. EQUIVALENT\nOld Sections 82, 83, 84, 85, 86 and 87, Cr.P.C., 1973 -> Sections 84 to 89, BNSS, 2023.\nPRECEDENTS REFERRED TO\n• State of M.P. v. Pradeep Sharma, (2014) 2 SCC 171 - the object of the proclamation and the prerequisites: the person must be\nshown to be absconding with the intention of evading the process of law.\n• Devendra Singh v. State of U.P., (2019) 10 SCC 106 - the publication of the proclamation; the process must comply with the\nrequirements of law.\n• Vijay Kumar Dhama v. State of U.P., 2022 SCC OnLine SC 468 - the conditions precedent for the issuance of a proclamation\nunder Section 82, Cr.P.C.\n• Ramesh Kumar v. State of U.P., 2020 SCC OnLine SC 1097 - the attachment of the property of an absconding accused; the\nsafeguards."
